module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Divisors.CanonicalSectionRegular
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Divisors.ZeroSchemeIdealSheaf
public import StacksCore.Tactic.StacksAttribute
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Modules.FreeSections

/-!
# Effective Cartier divisors and regular sections of invertible modules

Let `X` be a scheme. An effective Cartier divisor `D ⊆ X` carries the invertible module
`𝒪_X(D) = ℐ_D^∨` together with its canonical section `1_D`, and `1_D` is a regular section
([Stacks, Tag 01WY]). Conversely an invertible module `L` with a regular global section `s` has
for its zero scheme `Z(s)` ([Stacks, Tag 02OQ]) an effective Cartier divisor, and there is a
*unique* isomorphism `𝒪_X(Z(s)) ≅ L` carrying `1_{Z(s)}` to `s`. The two constructions are
mutually inverse bijections between the effective Cartier divisors on `X` and the isomorphism
classes of pairs `(L, s)`.

Uniqueness of the isomorphism is the statement that an endomorphism of an invertible module
fixing a regular global section is the identity. Over a trivializing affine open an endomorphism
is multiplication by a function, the section is a nonzerodivisor function times the generator, and
cancelling it forces the multiplier to be `1`; the identity then holds over every open because a
sheaf is separated.

Well-definedness of the inverse map needs the zero scheme to be an invariant of the isomorphism
class: the defining ideal of `Z(s)` on an affine open is generated by the values of the linear
functionals at `s`, and an isomorphism of pairs matches those functionals up.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.sectionsLinearMap`: the action on sections of a morphism of
  `𝒪_X`-modules, as a linear map.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.EffectiveCartierDivisor`: an effective Cartier divisor, as a subtype of the
  quasi-coherent ideal-sheaf data on `X`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RegularSectionPair`: an invertible `𝒪_X`-module with a regular global
  section.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RegularSectionPair.IsIsomorphic` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RegularSectionClass`: isomorphism of such pairs, and the type of
  isomorphism classes.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RegularSectionPair.zeroScheme`: the zero scheme of the distinguished section
  of a pair.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.effectiveCartierDivisor_equiv_regularSectionClass`: the bijection of
  [Stacks, Tag 01X0].

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.zeroScheme_eq_of_iso`: the zero scheme only depends on the isomorphism class
  of the pair.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.eq_id_of_sectionsMap_eq`: an endomorphism of an invertible module fixing a
  regular global section is the identity.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.existsUnique_invertibleSheafIso_zeroScheme`: there is a unique isomorphism
  `𝒪_X(Z(s)) ≅ L` carrying the canonical section to `s`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.effectiveCartierDivisor_equiv_regularSectionClass_apply` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.effectiveCartierDivisor_equiv_regularSectionClass_symm_apply`: the two
  constructions realizing the bijection.

## Implementation notes

The relation on pairs is written as the existence of an isomorphism of the modules whose
*morphism from the structure sheaf* matches, that is `ŝ ≫ i.hom = t̂` for the morphisms
`ŝ : 𝒪_X ⟶ L` and `t̂ : 𝒪_X ⟶ M` of `SheafOfModules.unitHomEquiv`. That is the same condition as
`SheafOfModules.sectionsMap i.hom s = t` — the content of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.unitHomEquiv_symm_comp_eq_iff` — and the latter form is what
the proofs below use.

Several steps are done in term mode rather than by `rw`. The sheaf-of-modules index of a scheme
has two spellings, `X.ringCatSheaf` and the full-subcategory object of the sheaf condition on the
structure presheaf, and a goal mixing them is not type-correct at the `implicit` transparency
level, so `rw` refuses to fire on it; term elaboration runs at default transparency and accepts
the identification.

A global section is determined by its component on the whole space
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.sections_ext_of_eval_top`), which is what turns the
component-level identities produced by `AlgebraicGeometry.invertibleSheafIsoZeroScheme_section`
into equalities of sections.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01WX](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01WX)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01WY](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01WY)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01X0](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01X0)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 02OQ](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02OQ)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 02OR](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02OR)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory TopologicalSpace Opposite
open AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules (sectionsLinearMap sectionsLinearMap_apply)

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

variable {X : Scheme.{u}}

namespace Scheme.Modules

/-- **A global section of an `𝒪_X`-module is determined by its component on the whole space**,
the other components being its restrictions. -/
theorem sections_ext_of_eval_top {L : X.Modules} {s t : L.sections}
    (h : s.eval (op ⊤) = t.eval (op ⊤)) : s = t :=
  Subtype.ext (funext fun U =>
    (map_eval_top L s U.unop).symm.trans
      ((congrArg (fun y : Γ(L, ⊤) => L.presheaf.map (homOfLE (le_top : U.unop ≤ ⊤)).op y) h).trans
        (map_eval_top L t U.unop)))

/-- An isomorphism of `𝒪_X`-modules is bijective on sections, here in the form that its inverse
undoes it. -/
theorem app_inv_app {L M : X.Modules} (i : L ≅ M) (U : X.Opens) (x : Γ(L, U)) :
    Hom.app i.inv U (Hom.app i.hom U x) = x :=
  have h := congrArg (fun φ => Hom.app φ U x) i.hom_inv_id
  have h2 : Hom.app i.inv U (Hom.app i.hom U x) = x := h
  h2

/-- If an isomorphism carries `x` to `y`, its inverse carries `y` back to `x`. -/
private theorem app_inv_eq_of_app_eq {L M : X.Modules} (i : L ≅ M) {U : X.Opens} {x : Γ(L, U)}
    {y : Γ(M, U)} (h : Hom.app i.hom U x = y) : Hom.app i.inv U y = x :=
  (congrArg (Hom.app i.inv U) h).symm.trans (app_inv_app i U x)

/-- **Matching the morphisms from the structure sheaf is matching the sections.** For a morphism
`f : L ⟶ M` of `𝒪_X`-modules and global sections `s` of `L` and `t` of `M`, the morphism
`𝒪_X ⟶ L` of `s` composed with `f` is the morphism of `t` exactly when `f` carries `s` to `t`. -/
theorem unitHomEquiv_symm_comp_eq_iff {L M : X.Modules} (s : L.sections) (t : M.sections)
    (f : L ⟶ M) :
    L.unitHomEquiv.symm s ≫ f = M.unitHomEquiv.symm t ↔ SheafOfModules.sectionsMap f s = t :=
  ⟨fun h => M.unitHomEquiv.symm.injective
      ((SheafOfModules.unitHomEquiv_symm_comp s f).symm.trans h),
    fun h => (SheafOfModules.unitHomEquiv_symm_comp s f).trans
      (congrArg (fun u : M.sections => M.unitHomEquiv.symm u) h)⟩

end Scheme.Modules

/-! ### The zero scheme is an invariant of the isomorphism class -/

/-- **A morphism of pairs contracts the generators of the defining ideal of the zero scheme.**
The ideal of `Z(s)` over an open is generated by the values of the `Γ(X, U)`-linear functionals
on `Γ(L, U)` at `s|_U` ([Stacks, Tag 02OQ]); a morphism `f : L ⟶ M` carrying `s` to `t` turns a
functional on `Γ(M, U)` into one on `Γ(L, U)` with the same value. -/
theorem zeroScheme_generators_subset {L M : X.Modules} {s : Γ(L, ⊤)} {t : Γ(M, ⊤)} (f : L ⟶ M)
    (hf : Scheme.Modules.Hom.app f ⊤ s = t) (U : X.Opens) :
    {a : Γ(X, U) | ∃ ψ : Γ(M, U) →ₗ[Γ(X, U)] Γ(X, U),
        a = ψ (M.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op t)} ⊆
      {a : Γ(X, U) | ∃ φ : Γ(L, U) →ₗ[Γ(X, U)] Γ(X, U),
        a = φ (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s)} := by
  rintro a ⟨ψ, rfl⟩
  refine ⟨ψ ∘ₗ sectionsLinearMap f U, ?_⟩
  have hnat : Scheme.Modules.Hom.app f U (L.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op s) =
      M.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op (Scheme.Modules.Hom.app f ⊤ s) :=
    PresheafOfModules.naturality_apply f.val (homOfLE le_top).op s
  rw [LinearMap.comp_apply]
  exact congrArg ψ (hnat.trans
    (congrArg (fun y : Γ(M, ⊤) => M.presheaf.map (homOfLE le_top).op y) hf)).symm

/-- **The zero scheme only depends on the isomorphism class of the pair.** If an isomorphism
`i : L ≅ M` of invertible `𝒪_X`-modules carries the global section `s` to `t`, then `Z(s) = Z(t)`
([Stacks, Tag 02OQ]). -/
theorem zeroScheme_eq_of_iso {L M : X.Modules}
    [SheafOfModules.IsInvertible X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property L]
    [SheafOfModules.IsInvertible X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property M]
    {s : Γ(L, ⊤)} {t : Γ(M, ⊤)} (i : L ≅ M)
    (hi : Scheme.Modules.Hom.app i.hom ⊤ s = t) :
    zeroScheme X L s = zeroScheme X M t :=
  congrArg Scheme.IdealSheafData.ofIdeals (funext fun U =>
    congrArg Ideal.span (Set.Subset.antisymm
      (zeroScheme_generators_subset i.inv (Scheme.Modules.app_inv_eq_of_app_eq i hi) U.1)
      (zeroScheme_generators_subset i.hom hi U.1)))

/-! ### Endomorphisms fixing a regular section -/

section Endomorphism

variable (L : X.Modules)
  [SheafOfModules.IsInvertible X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property L]

omit [SheafOfModules.IsInvertible X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property L] in
/-- **Over a trivializing affine open, an endomorphism fixing a nonzerodivisor section is the
identity.** On such an open `Γ(L, V)` is free of rank one, so the endomorphism is multiplication
by a function `u`; the section is a nonzerodivisor function `c` times the generator
([Stacks, Tag 01WZ], `AlgebraicGeometry.isRegular_coordinate_of_isRegularSection`), and
`c * u = c` forces `u = 1`. -/
theorem appLinearMap_eq_self_of_trivialization (s : L.sections)
    (hs : IsRegularSection X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property s) {φ : L ⟶ L}
    (hφ : SheafOfModules.sectionsMap φ s = s)
    (V : X.affineOpens) (e : Γ(L, V.1) ≃ₗ[Γ(X, V.1)] Γ(X, V.1)) (x : Γ(L, V.1)) :
    Scheme.Modules.Hom.app φ V.1 x = x := by
  set α := sectionsLinearMap φ V.1 with hα
  set u := e (α (e.symm (1 : Γ(X, V.1)))) with hu
  have key : ∀ y : Γ(L, V.1), e (α y) = e y * u := fun y => by
    have hy : α y = e y • α (e.symm (1 : Γ(X, V.1))) := by
      rw [← map_smul]
      congr 1
      rw [← map_smul, smul_eq_mul, mul_one, e.symm_apply_apply]
    rw [hy, map_smul, smul_eq_mul, hu]
  have hreg : IsRegular (e (s.eval (op V.1))) := by
    have h := isRegular_coordinate_of_isRegularSection X L s hs V e
    rwa [Scheme.Modules.map_eval_top L s V.1] at h
  have hfix : α (s.eval (op V.1)) = s.eval (op V.1) :=
    congrArg (fun z : L.sections => z.eval (op V.1)) hφ
  have hcu : e (s.eval (op V.1)) * u = e (s.eval (op V.1)) * 1 :=
    (key (s.eval (op V.1))).symm.trans
      ((congrArg (fun y : Γ(L, V.1) => e y) hfix).trans (mul_one _).symm)
  exact e.injective ((key x).trans
    ((congrArg (fun r : Γ(X, V.1) => e x * r) (hreg.left hcu)).trans (mul_one _)))

/-- **An endomorphism of an invertible module fixing a regular global section acts as the
identity on the sections over every open.** The trivializing affine opens inside an open cover it
(`AlgebraicGeometry.exists_affineOpens_linearEquiv_le`), the endomorphism is the identity on each
of them, and a sheaf is separated. -/
theorem app_eq_self_of_sectionsMap_eq (s : L.sections)
    (hs : IsRegularSection X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property s) {φ : L ⟶ L}
    (hφ : SheafOfModules.sectionsMap φ s = s) (U : X.Opens) (x : Γ(L, U)) :
    Scheme.Modules.Hom.app φ U x = x := by
  classical
  choose V hxV hVU he using fun (y : X) (hy : y ∈ U) =>
    exists_affineOpens_linearEquiv_le X L y U hy
  refine TopCat.Sheaf.eq_of_locally_eq_of_isSheafPresheaf L.isSheaf
    (ι := {y : X // y ∈ U}) (fun k => (V k.1 k.2).1) (fun k => hVU k.1 k.2)
    (fun y hy => TopologicalSpace.Opens.mem_iSup.2 ⟨⟨y, hy⟩, hxV y hy⟩) fun k => ?_
  refine (PresheafOfModules.app_resLE φ.val (hVU k.1 k.2) x).symm.trans ?_
  exact appLinearMap_eq_self_of_trivialization L s hs hφ (V k.1 k.2) (he k.1 k.2).some _

/-- **An endomorphism of an invertible module fixing a regular global section is the identity.**
This is the uniqueness half of [Stacks, Tag 01X0] (2). -/
theorem eq_id_of_sectionsMap_eq (s : L.sections)
    (hs : IsRegularSection X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property s) {φ : L ⟶ L}
    (hφ : SheafOfModules.sectionsMap φ s = s) : φ = 𝟙 L :=
  SheafOfModules.Hom.ext (PresheafOfModules.hom_ext fun U =>
    ModuleCat.hom_ext (LinearMap.ext fun x =>
      app_eq_self_of_sectionsMap_eq L s hs hφ U.unop x))

end Endomorphism

/-! ### From a regular section to an effective Cartier divisor -/

add_to_stacks 01X0 "regular_section_divisor"
  AlgebraicGeometry.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_zeroScheme_iff_isRegularSection

/-- **There is a unique isomorphism from the invertible sheaf of the zero scheme of a regular
section to the original invertible sheaf that sends its canonical section to that section.**
This is [Stacks, Tag 01X0] (2): for `s` a regular section of an invertible `𝒪_X`-module `L` the
zero scheme `D = Z(s)` is an effective Cartier divisor
(`AlgebraicGeometry.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_zeroScheme_iff_isRegularSection`) and `𝒪_X(D) ≅ L`
by a unique isomorphism matching `1_D` with `s`.

Existence is `AlgebraicGeometry.invertibleSheafIsoZeroSchemeOfIsRegularSection` and its section
identity; uniqueness is `AlgebraicGeometry.eq_id_of_sectionsMap_eq`, applied to the endomorphism
of `L` built from two such isomorphisms. -/
@[stacks 01X0 "regular_section_divisor"]
theorem existsUnique_invertibleSheafIso_zeroScheme (X : Scheme.{u}) (L : X.Modules)
    [SheafOfModules.IsInvertible X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property L] (s : L.sections)
    (hs : IsRegularSection X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property s) :
    ∃! e : Stacks.Divisors.invertibleSheaf' (zeroScheme X L (s.eval (op ⊤))) ≅ L,
      Scheme.Modules.Hom.app e.hom ⊤
          ((Stacks.Divisors.canonicalSection'
            (zeroScheme X L (s.eval (op ⊤)))).eval (op ⊤)) = s.eval (op ⊤) := by
  set i := invertibleSheafIsoZeroSchemeOfIsRegularSection L s hs with hi
  have hsec := invertibleSheafIsoZeroSchemeOfIsRegularSection_section L s hs
  have hinv : Scheme.Modules.Hom.app i.inv ⊤
      ((Stacks.Divisors.canonicalSection' (zeroScheme X L (s.eval (op ⊤)))).eval (op ⊤)) =
      s.eval (op ⊤) :=
    Scheme.Modules.app_inv_eq_of_app_eq i hsec
  refine ⟨i.symm, hinv, fun e he => Iso.ext ?_⟩
  have hfix : Scheme.Modules.Hom.app (e.inv ≫ i.inv) ⊤ (s.eval (op ⊤)) = s.eval (op ⊤) :=
    (congrArg (Scheme.Modules.Hom.app i.inv ⊤) (Scheme.Modules.app_inv_eq_of_app_eq e he)).trans
      hinv
  have hid : e.inv ≫ i.inv = 𝟙 L :=
    eq_id_of_sectionsMap_eq L s hs (Scheme.Modules.sections_ext_of_eval_top hfix)
  have hA : e.hom ≫ (e.inv ≫ i.inv) = e.hom :=
    (congrArg (fun ψ : L ⟶ L => e.hom ≫ ψ) hid).trans (Category.comp_id e.hom)
  have hB : e.hom ≫ (e.inv ≫ i.inv) = i.inv :=
    (Category.assoc e.hom e.inv i.inv).symm.trans
      ((congrArg (fun ψ => ψ ≫ i.inv) e.hom_inv_id).trans (Category.id_comp i.inv))
  exact hA.symm.trans hB

/-! ### The bijection -/

/-- **An effective Cartier divisor on a scheme**, represented by its defining quasi-coherent
sheaf of ideals ([Stacks, Tag 01WR]). -/
abbrev EffectiveCartierDivisor (X : Scheme.{u}) :=
  {D : X.IdealSheafData // Stacks.Divisors.IsEffectiveCartierDivisor D}

/-- **An invertible `𝒪_X`-module together with a regular global section**
([Stacks, Tag 01WY]). -/
structure RegularSectionPair (X : Scheme.{u}) where
  /-- The module. -/
  module : X.Modules
  /-- Invertibility of the module. -/
  invertible : SheafOfModules.IsInvertible X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property module
  /-- The global section. -/
  s : module.sections
  /-- Regularity of the section. -/
  regular : IsRegularSection X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property s

/-- **Two regular-section pairs are isomorphic** when an isomorphism of their modules carries one
distinguished section to the other, written as an equality of the associated morphisms from the
structure sheaf. -/
def RegularSectionPair.IsIsomorphic (P Q : RegularSectionPair X) : Prop :=
  ∃ i : P.module ≅ Q.module,
    P.module.unitHomEquiv.symm P.s ≫ i.hom = Q.module.unitHomEquiv.symm Q.s

/-- Isomorphism of regular-section pairs is an equivalence relation: the identity, the inverse
and the composite of isomorphisms of modules carry the distinguished sections accordingly. -/
instance RegularSectionPair.setoid (X : Scheme.{u}) : Setoid (RegularSectionPair X) where
  r := RegularSectionPair.IsIsomorphic
  iseqv := by
    refine ⟨fun P => ⟨Iso.refl P.module, ?_⟩, ?_, ?_⟩
    · exact (Scheme.Modules.unitHomEquiv_symm_comp_eq_iff P.s P.s (Iso.refl P.module).hom).mpr rfl
    · rintro P Q ⟨i, hi⟩
      refine ⟨i.symm, (Scheme.Modules.unitHomEquiv_symm_comp_eq_iff Q.s P.s i.inv).mpr ?_⟩
      have h := (Scheme.Modules.unitHomEquiv_symm_comp_eq_iff P.s Q.s i.hom).mp hi
      have hundo := congrArg (fun φ => SheafOfModules.sectionsMap φ P.s) i.hom_inv_id
      have hundo' : SheafOfModules.sectionsMap i.inv
          (SheafOfModules.sectionsMap i.hom P.s) = P.s := hundo
      exact (congrArg (SheafOfModules.sectionsMap i.inv) h).symm.trans hundo'
    · rintro P Q R ⟨i, hi⟩ ⟨j, hj⟩
      refine ⟨i ≪≫ j, (Scheme.Modules.unitHomEquiv_symm_comp_eq_iff P.s R.s (i ≪≫ j).hom).mpr ?_⟩
      have h := (Scheme.Modules.unitHomEquiv_symm_comp_eq_iff P.s Q.s i.hom).mp hi
      have h' := (Scheme.Modules.unitHomEquiv_symm_comp_eq_iff Q.s R.s j.hom).mp hj
      have hcomp : SheafOfModules.sectionsMap (i ≪≫ j).hom P.s =
          SheafOfModules.sectionsMap j.hom (SheafOfModules.sectionsMap i.hom P.s) := rfl
      exact hcomp.trans ((congrArg (SheafOfModules.sectionsMap j.hom) h).trans h')

/-- **The isomorphism classes of invertible `𝒪_X`-modules with a regular global section.** -/
abbrev RegularSectionClass (X : Scheme.{u}) := Quotient (RegularSectionPair.setoid X)

/-- The isomorphism class of a regular-section pair. -/
abbrev RegularSectionPair.classOf (P : RegularSectionPair X) : RegularSectionClass X :=
  Quotient.mk _ P

/-- **The zero scheme of the distinguished section of a regular-section pair**
([Stacks, Tag 02OQ]). -/
abbrev RegularSectionPair.zeroScheme (P : RegularSectionPair X) : X.IdealSheafData :=
  @AlgebraicGeometry.zeroScheme X P.module P.invertible (P.s.eval (op ⊤))

/-- **The zero scheme of the distinguished section of a regular-section pair is an effective
Cartier divisor** ([Stacks, Tag 02OR] (4)). -/
theorem RegularSectionPair.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_zeroScheme (P : RegularSectionPair X) :
    Stacks.Divisors.IsEffectiveCartierDivisor P.zeroScheme :=
  (@isEffectiveCartierDivisor_zeroScheme_iff_isRegularSection X P.module P.invertible
    P.s).mpr P.regular

/-- **Isomorphic regular-section pairs have the same zero scheme**, so the zero scheme is a
function of the isomorphism class. -/
theorem RegularSectionPair.zeroScheme_eq_of_isIsomorphic {P Q : RegularSectionPair X}
    (h : P.IsIsomorphic Q) : P.zeroScheme = Q.zeroScheme := by
  obtain ⟨i, hi⟩ := h
  have hs := (Scheme.Modules.unitHomEquiv_symm_comp_eq_iff P.s Q.s i.hom).mp hi
  have htop := congrArg (fun z : Q.module.sections => z.eval (op ⊤)) hs
  have htop' : Scheme.Modules.Hom.app i.hom ⊤ (P.s.eval (op ⊤)) = Q.s.eval (op ⊤) := htop
  exact @zeroScheme_eq_of_iso X P.module Q.module P.invertible Q.invertible _ _ i htop'

/-- **The regular-section pair of an effective Cartier divisor**: its invertible sheaf `𝒪_X(D)`
([Stacks, Tag 01WX]) with the canonical section `1_D`, which is regular
(`Stacks.Divisors.canonicalSection_isRegular`). -/
@[ega "IV 21.3.3" "(ii)"]
def EffectiveCartierDivisor.regularSectionPair (D : EffectiveCartierDivisor X) :
    RegularSectionPair X where
  module := Stacks.Divisors.invertibleSheaf' D.1
  invertible := @Stacks.Divisors.isInvertible_divisorSheaf X D.1 D.2
  s := Stacks.Divisors.canonicalSection' D.1
  regular := @Stacks.Divisors.canonicalSection_isRegular X D.1 D.2

/-- **The zero scheme of the canonical section of the invertible sheaf of an effective Cartier
divisor is that divisor**, so the forward construction of [Stacks, Tag 01X0] is a section of the
backward one. This is `Stacks.Divisors.zeroScheme_canonicalSection`. -/
theorem EffectiveCartierDivisor.zeroScheme_regularSectionPair (D : EffectiveCartierDivisor X) :
    D.regularSectionPair.zeroScheme = D.1 :=
  @Stacks.Divisors.zeroScheme_canonicalSection X D.1 D.2

/-- **An invertible module with a regular global section is isomorphic, as a pair, to the
invertible sheaf of the zero scheme of that section with its canonical section**, so the backward
construction of [Stacks, Tag 01X0] is a section of the forward one. The isomorphism is
`AlgebraicGeometry.invertibleSheafIsoZeroSchemeOfIsRegularSection`. -/
theorem RegularSectionPair.isIsomorphic_regularSectionPair (P : RegularSectionPair X) :
    RegularSectionPair.IsIsomorphic
      (EffectiveCartierDivisor.regularSectionPair
        ⟨P.zeroScheme, P.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_zeroScheme⟩) P := by
  have := P.invertible
  refine ⟨(invertibleSheafIsoZeroSchemeOfIsRegularSection P.module P.s P.regular).symm, ?_⟩
  refine (Scheme.Modules.unitHomEquiv_symm_comp_eq_iff _ P.s _).mpr
    (Scheme.Modules.sections_ext_of_eval_top ?_)
  exact Scheme.Modules.app_inv_eq_of_app_eq
    (invertibleSheafIsoZeroSchemeOfIsRegularSection P.module P.s P.regular)
    (invertibleSheafIsoZeroSchemeOfIsRegularSection_section P.module P.s P.regular)

/-- **The canonical bijection between the effective Cartier divisors on a scheme and the
isomorphism classes of invertible modules with a regular global section**
([Stacks, Tag 01X0]).

Forwards a divisor `D` goes to the class of `(𝒪_X(D), 1_D)`; backwards a pair `(L, s)` goes to
the zero scheme `Z(s)`, which is well defined on classes by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RegularSectionPair.zeroScheme_eq_of_isIsomorphic`. The two round trips are
`Z(1_D) = D` (`Stacks.Divisors.zeroScheme_canonicalSection`) and
`(𝒪_X(Z(s)), 1_{Z(s)}) ≅ (L, s)`
(`AlgebraicGeometry.invertibleSheafIsoZeroSchemeOfIsRegularSection`). -/
@[stacks 01X0 "divisor_regular_section_equiv", ega "IV 21.3.3" "(ii)"]
def effectiveCartierDivisor_equiv_regularSectionClass (X : Scheme.{u}) :
    EffectiveCartierDivisor X ≃ RegularSectionClass X where
  toFun D := (EffectiveCartierDivisor.regularSectionPair D).classOf
  invFun := Quotient.lift
    (fun P : RegularSectionPair X =>
      (⟨P.zeroScheme, P.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_zeroScheme⟩ : EffectiveCartierDivisor X))
    fun _ _ h => Subtype.ext (RegularSectionPair.zeroScheme_eq_of_isIsomorphic h)
  left_inv D := Subtype.ext D.zeroScheme_regularSectionPair
  right_inv := Quotient.ind fun P =>
    Quotient.sound P.isIsomorphic_regularSectionPair

/-- **The forward map of the bijection of [Stacks, Tag 01X0] sends an effective Cartier divisor
to the isomorphism class of its invertible sheaf with its canonical section**, which is a regular
section. -/
@[stacks 01X0 "divisor_regular_section_equiv"]
theorem effectiveCartierDivisor_equiv_regularSectionClass_apply (D : EffectiveCartierDivisor X) :
    ∃ h : IsRegularSection X.sheaf.obj X.ringCatSheaf.property
        (Stacks.Divisors.canonicalSection' D.1),
      effectiveCartierDivisor_equiv_regularSectionClass X D =
        RegularSectionPair.classOf
          ⟨Stacks.Divisors.invertibleSheaf' D.1,
            @Stacks.Divisors.isInvertible_divisorSheaf X D.1 D.2,
            Stacks.Divisors.canonicalSection' D.1, h⟩ :=
  ⟨@Stacks.Divisors.canonicalSection_isRegular X D.1 D.2, rfl⟩

/-- **The inverse map of the bijection of [Stacks, Tag 01X0] sends the class of a regular-section
pair to the effective Cartier divisor cut out by its zero scheme.** The equation holds for every
representative of the class. -/
@[stacks 01X0 "divisor_regular_section_equiv"]
theorem effectiveCartierDivisor_equiv_regularSectionClass_symm_apply (P : RegularSectionPair X) :
    ∃ h : Stacks.Divisors.IsEffectiveCartierDivisor P.zeroScheme,
      (effectiveCartierDivisor_equiv_regularSectionClass X).symm P.classOf = ⟨P.zeroScheme, h⟩ :=
  ⟨P.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_zeroScheme, rfl⟩

end AlgebraicGeometry

